      "subject": "BtsT",
      "text": "Additionally, the specificity of BtsT was tested by assaying several compounds with shared structural similarity for the ability to act as competitive inhibitors for the pyruvate binding site when provided in a 100-fold access (Fig. 4B). Br-pyruvate is a synthetic analogue of pyruvate, and was found to act as a competitive inhibitor of BtsT, which is in agreement with earlier studies in E. coli K-12 (16). None of the other compounds used (l-malate, l-lactate, and d-lactate with a C-α hydroxyl group; phosphoenolpyruvate [PEP] with a C-α phosphoryl group; or l-alanine with a positively charged C-α amino group) reduced the rate of pyruvate uptake. We also tested monocarboxylates (acetate and propionate), a dicarboxylate (succinate), and amino acids (l-serine and l-glycine), but the uptake rate was significantly reduced only in the presence of pyruvate or its synthetic analog Br-pyruvate, indicating the narrow specificity of the transporter. In order to determine the Km for pyruvate uptake, we quantified the initial rate of pyruvate uptake by BtsT at different concentrations of pyruvate (Fig. 4C). Pyruvate can enter the cells by diffusion (Fig. S8). Therefore, the uptake rates were corrected by the values determined for pure diffusion. The Km of the transporter BtsT for pyruvate measured in intact cells was 16.5 ± 6.4 μM."
    },
    "status": "completed"
  },
